7-Eleven confirmed a breach of systems storing franchisee documents, not ShinyHunters' claim of 600,000+ Salesforce records: its notification letters date the access to 2026-04-08, the filings in Massachusetts, Maine and Vermont count 47, 2 and 1 affected people, and 7-Eleven did not respond when asked about the 600,000-record claim ([Security Affairs, 2026-05-18](https://securityaffairs.com/192336/data-breach/shinyhunters-hack-7-eleven-franchisee-data-and-salesforce-records-exposed.html); [Cybersecurity Dive, 2026-05-20](https://www.cybersecuritydive.com/news/7-eleven-cyberattack-franchisee-data/820698/)). SecurityWeek lists Instructure, Vimeo, Wynn Resorts, Vercel and Medtronic as recent claims by ShinyHunters and affiliated actors and describes the group's Salesforce intrusions since mid-2025 as phishing, third-party-integration abuse or misconfigurations; it does not place those victims in one campaign with 7-Eleven or name a vector for the 7-Eleven breach ([SecurityWeek, 2026-05-18](https://www.securityweek.com/7-eleven-data-breach-confirmed-after-shinyhunters-ransom-demand/)). Likewise, Charter did not disclose how the attackers got in, and CyberInsider only links the Charter claim to the Salesforce-focused campaign ([CyberInsider, 2026-05-23](https://cyberinsider.com/charter-communications-confirms-data-breach-as-hackers-threaten-leak-of-42-million-records/)).
